Setting (Section 4.2). $O_u:V\to[n]$ orders the vertices by their $G$-distance to $u$ (ties arbitrary), so $O_u(x)=j$ means $x$ is the $j$-th closest vertex to $u$. For $r\in[t]$ and $i\in[k-2]$: $A^r_{k-1}$ is one uniformly random vertex, and for each level the set $A^r_i$ is obtained from $A^r_{i+1}$ by adding $n^{1-(i+1)/k}-n^{1-(i+2)/k}$ further vertices sampled uniformly WITHOUT replacement from $V\setminus A^r_{i+1}$; hence $A^r_{i+1}\subseteq A^r_i$, $|A^r_i|=n^{1-(i+1)/k}$, and $A_{i+1}=\bigcup_{r'\in[t]}A^{r'}_{i+1}$ (so $A^r_{i+1}\subseteq A_i^r\cap A_{i+1}$). Lemma 4.3 gives $\mathbb{P}[u\in A^r_i]=|A^r_i|/n$ for every vertex $u$.

The chain to check is the proof of Lemma 4.5:
(a) for a vertex $v\in A_{i+1}$, $\mathbb{P}[O_u(v)=j]=1/n$ for every $j\in[n]$;
(b) given $O_u(v)=j$, the probability that every $y\in A^r_i\setminus\{w\}$ has $O_u(y)>j$ is $(1-j/n)^{|A^r_i|-1}$;
(c) hence $\mathbb{P}[d(u,v)\le d(u,A^r_i\setminus\{w\})] = \sum_{j=1}^{n}(1-j/n)^{|A^r_i|-1}\frac1n \ \ge \int_{1/n}^{1}(1-x)^{|A^r_i|-1}dx = (1-1/n)^{|A^r_i|}/|A^r_i| \ \ge\ 1/(2|A^r_i|)$;
(d) $\mathbb{P}[d(u,p_{i+1}(u))\le d(u,A^r_i\setminus\{w\})] \ \ge\ 1-\left(1-\frac{1}{2|A^r_i|}\right)^{|A_{i+1}|}$.

Points to examine: (b) raises a per-vertex probability to the power $|A^r_i|-1$, i.e. treats the members of $A^r_i\setminus\{w\}$ as independent uniform draws, although they are sampled without replacement and conditioned on containing $A^r_{i+1}$; and (d) raises a per-vertex success probability to the power $|A_{i+1}|$, i.e. treats the events '$v$ precedes all of $A^r_i\setminus\{w\}$' for the different $v\in A_{i+1}$ as mutually independent, although they are all events about the same random set $A^r_i$ and the sets $A_{i+1}$ and $A^r_i$ overlap by construction ($A^r_{i+1}\subseteq A^r_i\cap A_{i+1}$).
